00:45:59.120 --> 00:46:02.670
and you're lost, you're saying
these really damaging things

00:46:02.670 --> 00:46:04.453
to kids about their certainty.

00:46:05.530 --> 00:46:08.840
You're making their
eternal salvation be based

00:46:08.840 --> 00:46:12.750
on a one time event that
they had to get just right.

00:46:12.750 --> 00:46:16.480
And I had prayed the Sinner's
Prayer and done the whole,

00:46:16.480 --> 00:46:19.490
I want Jesus in my heart
and forgive me of my sins

00:46:19.490 --> 00:46:20.970
when I was like five years old.

00:46:20.970 --> 00:46:22.840
So for a lot of times
in the back of my mind,

00:46:22.840 --> 00:46:24.790
I was like, did I really understand it?

00:46:24.790 --> 00:46:29.773
And I remember being at a
Christian concert, NewSong,

00:46:31.560 --> 00:46:34.340
who came to the D. Rich
Auditorium at Campbell University

00:46:34.340 --> 00:46:35.760
where we saw many concerts,

00:46:35.760 --> 00:46:38.520
also saw a hypnotist one time, and I also

00:46:40.350 --> 00:46:42.460
heavy-petted my girlfriend

00:46:42.460 --> 00:46:43.950
in the balcony of this same place,

00:46:43.950 --> 00:46:44.947
just to put things in context.

00:46:44.947 --> 00:46:46.110
But not in the same

00:46:46.110 --> 00:46:47.204
night that you were dedicated.
Not the same night.

00:46:47.204 --> 00:46:50.570
Not the same night.

00:46:50.570 --> 00:46:52.263
I was not at this NewSong concert.

00:46:52.263 --> 00:46:54.920
Oh boy, you missed a
show, you missed a show.

00:46:54.920 --> 00:46:56.590
My mom actually was-

00:46:56.590 --> 00:46:59.230
Five white guys with
goatees singing in harmony?

00:46:59.230 --> 00:47:00.530
Yeah, and they're still around.

00:47:00.530 --> 00:47:02.150
NewSong is this, they're like Menudo,

00:47:02.150 --> 00:47:03.460
they just replace the members.

00:47:03.460 --> 00:47:05.183
Oh, well, it's gotta stay new.

00:47:06.610 --> 00:47:08.590
So you went for a rededication?

00:47:08.590 --> 00:47:11.460
No I didn't, again, I've always been,

00:47:11.460 --> 00:47:13.290
I'm more timid than you might think, okay?

00:47:13.290 --> 00:47:15.010
I'm more timid than you might think.

00:47:15.010 --> 00:47:15.843
It takes a lot of,

00:47:15.843 --> 00:47:17.510
you gotta check your pride to go forward.

00:47:17.510 --> 00:47:19.570
Well, the thing is, is that what happened

00:47:19.570 --> 00:47:23.103
was they were like, raise
your hand and come forward.

00:47:23.103 --> 00:47:26.240
It's a classic invitation
where at the end of a concert,

00:47:26.240 --> 00:47:27.640
you haven't really done the concert

00:47:27.640 --> 00:47:28.860
if you don't invite everybody

00:47:28.860 --> 00:47:32.410
to make a personal decision
for Jesus right then and there.

00:47:32.410 --> 00:47:36.250
And I've got this overwhelming
feeling that now is the time,

00:47:36.250 --> 00:47:39.843
I gotta just make sure, I
gotta make sure that I'm saved.

00:47:41.350 --> 00:47:43.630
I couldn't go forward because I was like,

00:47:43.630 --> 00:47:45.110
I knew so many people
there and I was like,

00:47:45.110 --> 00:47:46.523
I don't wanna confuse them.

00:47:47.500 --> 00:47:49.410
What are they gonna think about me?

00:47:49.410 --> 00:47:52.100
So on the way home with
my parents in the car

00:47:52.100 --> 00:47:54.173
is when I said, I started
crying and I was like,

00:47:54.173 --> 00:47:56.537
I don't think, I don't know if I'm saved

00:47:56.537 --> 00:47:58.580
and I really wanted to go forward.

00:47:58.580 --> 00:47:59.980
Well, this is like seventh grade?

00:47:59.980 --> 00:48:00.940
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:48:00.940 --> 00:48:02.150
Damn, you're like crying in the car?

00:48:02.150 --> 00:48:03.320
Yeah.

00:48:03.320 --> 00:48:05.410
'Cause maybe we talked about it,

00:48:05.410 --> 00:48:08.530
but I don't think we talked about it.

00:48:08.530 --> 00:48:12.100
You might have said I rededicated my life.

00:48:12.100 --> 00:48:15.390
Before that night, I remember that summer

00:48:15.390 --> 00:48:17.340
'cause I think that that concert was,

00:48:17.340 --> 00:48:18.950
it was either the beginning of seventh

00:48:18.950 --> 00:48:20.910
or beginning of eighth grade,
I can't remember the exact.

00:48:20.910 --> 00:48:25.220
I just remember that
summer, I couldn't go,

00:48:25.220 --> 00:48:27.020
and this is where I have
sympathy with people

00:48:27.020 --> 00:48:29.210
who have obsessive compulsive disorder

00:48:29.210 --> 00:48:30.993
and get obsessed about
one particular thing

00:48:30.993 --> 00:48:33.493
that maybe they think they
got wrong or they need.

00:48:35.300 --> 00:48:38.220
Having this thing that
I kept going back to

00:48:38.220 --> 00:48:40.350
that the moment that
my mind would move off

00:48:40.350 --> 00:48:42.610
of whatever I was directly engaged in;

00:48:42.610 --> 00:48:44.940
playing a video game,
shooting a basketball,

00:48:44.940 --> 00:48:46.630
boom, I would be hit with this.

00:48:46.630 --> 00:48:48.040
Oh man, I might die and go to hell

00:48:48.040 --> 00:48:49.540
and I've gotta get this right.

00:48:49.540 --> 00:48:51.510
Wow.
And it was overwhelming.

00:48:51.510 --> 00:48:52.550
It was really, again,

00:48:52.550 --> 00:48:54.840
I know I don't have obsessive
compulsive disorder,

00:48:54.840 --> 00:48:57.690
but it was this one time where I was like,

00:48:57.690 --> 00:48:59.500
now that I've grown up and I understand,

00:48:59.500 --> 00:49:01.360
I know people like my wife who have it,

00:49:01.360 --> 00:49:02.928
it's like, oh man.

00:49:02.928 --> 00:49:04.490
But for you there was a buildup to it,

00:49:04.490 --> 00:49:09.073
and did that NewSong night give
you the relief you desired?

00:49:10.230 --> 00:49:11.770
Yeah, I never thought about it again.

00:49:11.770 --> 00:49:13.050
Sealed the deal.

00:49:13.050 --> 00:49:15.220
'Cause then I was like, I
don't know when I got saved,

00:49:15.220 --> 00:49:16.640
if it was five or 14.

00:49:16.640 --> 00:49:18.090
And then as I got older,

00:49:18.090 --> 00:49:21.630
I began to understand more about grace

00:49:21.630 --> 00:49:23.970
and understand that like it's,

00:49:23.970 --> 00:49:25.180
do you think God was sitting,

00:49:25.180 --> 00:49:26.160
and if I was that worried about it,

00:49:26.160 --> 00:49:27.760
it means I was probably saved.

00:49:27.760 --> 00:49:29.640
Now, of course, I don't believe

00:49:29.640 --> 00:49:32.038
any of that framework anymore.

00:49:32.038 --> 00:49:33.503
But at the time I very much believed it,

00:49:33.503 --> 00:49:35.580
and it was the most important thing.

00:49:35.580 --> 00:49:37.738
Well, it does go to show you, I mean yeah.

00:49:37.738 --> 00:49:41.290
In our involvement at church
and in the youth group

00:49:41.290 --> 00:49:44.860
and in the choir, they were Sunday morning

00:49:44.860 --> 00:49:47.160
for Sunday school and church,

00:49:47.160 --> 00:49:50.110
Wednesday night was youth night,

00:49:50.110 --> 00:49:52.590
Thursday night was like the choir night.

00:49:52.590 --> 00:49:54.490
It's like, we were still
involved in all these things,

00:49:54.490 --> 00:49:59.093
and Tim was our youth pastor.

00:50:02.250 --> 00:50:04.610
He was bubbling over
with joy for all this.

00:50:04.610 --> 00:50:08.115
He was like a super
sweet, nice, sincere guy.

00:50:08.115 --> 00:50:12.340
He was enthusiastically
in love with Jesus.

00:50:12.340 --> 00:50:17.340
And enthusiastically,
all-systems-go on evangelism.

00:50:17.970 --> 00:50:22.130
And between him and Benny
Endsor who was involved,

00:50:22.130 --> 00:50:24.140
he was our choir director back then.

00:50:24.140 --> 00:50:25.240
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
Like middle school.

00:50:25.240 --> 00:50:27.722
Right.
And you know, we were like,

00:50:27.722 --> 00:50:30.190
so this is after "The Bible
Tells Me So Show" stuff,

00:50:30.190 --> 00:50:32.800
which basically, update on that,

00:50:32.800 --> 00:50:34.600
we have the-
You found it.

00:50:34.600 --> 00:50:38.110
We have the church play digitized

00:50:38.110 --> 00:50:40.890
and we're gonna find the right time for us

00:50:40.890 --> 00:50:45.690
to do like a participant commentary.

00:50:45.690 --> 00:50:48.820
We didn't direct it, on
the Mythical Society,

00:50:48.820 --> 00:50:50.550
'cause I think with the
rights that's the best,

00:50:50.550 --> 00:50:52.358
maybe the only, place we can do it.

00:50:52.358 --> 00:50:53.556
So we'll let you know when that happens.

00:50:53.556 --> 00:50:54.390
Do you think you're gonna get sued

00:50:54.390 --> 00:50:56.962
by "The Bible Tells Me So Show" people?

00:50:56.962 --> 00:50:57.795
Boy, they are.

00:50:57.795 --> 00:50:58.810
You think they're still doing that show?

00:50:58.810 --> 00:50:59.850
Boy, they are.

00:50:59.850 --> 00:51:00.780
They're very litigious?

00:51:00.780 --> 00:51:02.650
Yeah, they might be, I don't know.

00:51:02.650 --> 00:51:05.100
So this was the age of,

00:51:05.100 --> 00:51:06.790
by the time you get in
seventh and eighth grade,

00:51:06.790 --> 00:51:10.220
you can start to go on
mission trips, baby!

00:51:10.220 --> 00:51:11.970
Oh yeah.
That was huge for us

00:51:11.970 --> 00:51:13.680
because not only were we,

00:51:13.680 --> 00:51:15.840
all the stuff that we
had been doing at church

00:51:15.840 --> 00:51:18.883
and like how tight-knit
our group of friends was

00:51:18.883 --> 00:51:22.650
that had come up through
all of the indoctrination.

00:51:22.650 --> 00:51:27.020
Now we were going out, we're
taking trips in the summer

00:51:27.020 --> 00:51:30.210
and you didn't wanna feel too
guilty about that last day

00:51:30.210 --> 00:51:31.840
where it felt like a vacation

00:51:31.840 --> 00:51:33.800
because we were going
there for the Lord's work.

00:51:33.800 --> 00:51:35.660
After all, it's called a mission trip.

00:51:35.660 --> 00:51:38.850
You're on a mission to
bring the love of Jesus

00:51:38.850 --> 00:51:41.280
and salvation to the
people of the mountains

00:51:41.280 --> 00:51:45.470
of North Carolina or the
mountains of West Virginia or

00:51:45.470 --> 00:51:48.400
Trinidad.

00:51:48.400 --> 00:51:49.500
That's an island.

00:51:49.500 --> 00:51:52.080
I've never been out of the
continental United States,

00:51:52.080 --> 00:51:52.950
and here I am in-
That was the

00:51:52.950 --> 00:51:53.836
first time, yeah.

00:51:53.836 --> 00:51:56.940
I think that was after
our eighth grade year.

00:51:56.940 --> 00:51:59.920
But all of these mission trips were like,

00:51:59.920 --> 00:52:02.870
especially Trinidad,
these are like big moments

00:52:02.870 --> 00:52:05.903
of all of us taking this
huge risk where it's like,

00:52:05.903 --> 00:52:08.348
what is Tim gonna ask us to do?

00:52:08.348 --> 00:52:10.710
He's gonna ask us to like-
He was throwing accelerant

00:52:10.710 --> 00:52:13.543
on the spark of your faith, right?

00:52:16.150 --> 00:52:18.500
The honest perspective on this,

00:52:18.500 --> 00:52:20.190
and every youth pastor knows this,

00:52:20.190 --> 00:52:23.410
is that these mission trips are not really

00:52:23.410 --> 00:52:24.960
about the work that you're doing,

00:52:24.960 --> 00:52:26.190
and they're not really about the people

00:52:26.190 --> 00:52:27.630
that you're reaching
'cause most of the time

00:52:27.630 --> 00:52:29.280
you're just talking to
people who already know.

00:52:29.280 --> 00:52:30.930
And sure, I mean, yes,
there was a little boy

00:52:30.930 --> 00:52:32.640
who I used a bracelet to bring to

00:52:32.640 --> 00:52:36.060
a personal saving knowledge
of Jesus Christ in Trinidad.

00:52:36.060 --> 00:52:36.893
You used a bracelet?

00:52:36.893 --> 00:52:38.017
Like you bribed, you said,

00:52:38.017 --> 00:52:38.850
"I'll give you this bracelet

00:52:38.850 --> 00:52:39.957
if you accept Jesus."
No, no, no, no,

00:52:39.957 --> 00:52:42.030
we had the bracelet with the beads on it

00:52:42.030 --> 00:52:44.070
and each bead represented
a point in the gospel.

00:52:44.070 --> 00:52:45.000
Do you remember what was,

00:52:45.000 --> 00:52:50.000
the first bead was black, for sin.

00:52:50.950 --> 00:52:54.890
The second bead was
red for blood of Jesus.

00:52:54.890 --> 00:52:56.230
The next bead was white

00:52:56.230 --> 00:52:58.440
for Jesus cleanses your sins.

00:52:58.440 --> 00:53:00.390
Pretty racist, by the way.

00:53:00.390 --> 00:53:01.870
And then there's-
Black before sin,

00:53:01.870 --> 00:53:04.780
white after sin, but you know,
we didn't pick up on that.

00:53:04.780 --> 00:53:06.830
We were also talking to kids in Trinidad.

00:53:08.300 --> 00:53:09.495
I think there was a-
But still worked!

00:53:09.495 --> 00:53:12.030
I think there was a green one for growth?

00:53:12.030 --> 00:53:14.720
Yeah, there was a blue one for baptism.

00:53:14.720 --> 00:53:16.880
Because blue is baptism.

00:53:16.880 --> 00:53:20.900
That was after the white
was, 'cause you get saved

00:53:20.900 --> 00:53:23.310
and then you make a decision for Jesus.

00:53:23.310 --> 00:53:26.460
You make a public decision with baptism

00:53:26.460 --> 00:53:27.812
and then green, you grow.

00:53:27.812 --> 00:53:29.153
I think there was a yellow at some point.

00:53:29.153 --> 00:53:31.980
I think yellow was the rededication.

00:53:31.980 --> 00:53:33.480
The inevitable rededication.

00:53:35.730 --> 00:53:38.460
The point I'm making is
that it's really about

00:53:38.460 --> 00:53:43.020
the way it solidifies and
accelerates your own faith.

00:53:43.020 --> 00:53:45.930
It's those milestones that
if you want your youth group

00:53:45.930 --> 00:53:47.780
to like suddenly be on fire,

00:53:47.780 --> 00:53:50.870
you go somewhere together
and you basically have

00:53:50.870 --> 00:53:55.229
a trauma bond experience
together and then you're all in!

00:53:55.229 --> 00:53:57.330
Yeah, you're going on
this trip to like this

00:53:57.330 --> 00:53:58.540
Christian camps do
foreign island.

00:53:58.540 --> 00:53:59.470
the same thing.

00:53:59.470 --> 00:54:04.470
But Christian camp is, you're
not doing as much ministry.

00:54:04.610 --> 00:54:06.480
This was all, it was all outward oriented

00:54:06.480 --> 00:54:09.450
so like you were put in these
like outta your comfort zone.

00:54:09.450 --> 00:54:14.130
Every day you would go and
let's paint this woman's house.

00:54:14.130 --> 00:54:15.960
And I have a scar on
my knee where I stepped

00:54:15.960 --> 00:54:17.630
through a barbed wire
fence and like ripped out

00:54:17.630 --> 00:54:19.400
a chunk of my knee.

00:54:19.400 --> 00:54:21.880
I always remember that
trip to the mountains

00:54:21.880 --> 00:54:24.270
of North Carolina, painting
that woman's house.

00:54:24.270 --> 00:54:26.890
And I remember some of us was like,

00:54:26.890 --> 00:54:28.660
we gotta go up to the
door and talk to her,

00:54:28.660 --> 00:54:31.073
like, we gotta make sure
that she knows Jesus.

00:54:32.020 --> 00:54:34.650
And just kind of having this
sense of mission and obligation

00:54:34.650 --> 00:54:37.800
and you know, you're serving people,

00:54:37.800 --> 00:54:40.120
but you're also trying to seal the deal.

00:54:40.120 --> 00:54:41.673
And yeah, you either had a bracelet

00:54:41.673 --> 00:54:43.240
or you would be taught to,

00:54:43.240 --> 00:54:45.620
you could use the five
fingers on your hand.

00:54:45.620 --> 00:54:46.453
Yeah, that's cool.

00:54:46.453 --> 00:54:50.067
So it's like, put your
thumb up, thumbs up.

00:54:50.067 --> 00:54:52.270
Good news.
I've got good news.

00:54:52.270 --> 00:54:53.570
Point your index finger.

00:54:53.570 --> 00:54:54.530
You are gonna die!

00:54:54.530 --> 00:54:55.813
You're going to hell.

00:54:57.410 --> 00:54:59.010
And then middle finger.

00:54:59.010 --> 00:55:00.110
I guess middle finger would be

00:55:00.110 --> 00:55:01.130
going to hell.
Going to hell.

00:55:01.130 --> 00:55:03.320
I think you it's like, so I don't know,

00:55:03.320 --> 00:55:05.200
those first two are like,
you're going to hell.

00:55:05.200 --> 00:55:06.887
See? Bird.
Yeah, right.

00:55:06.887 --> 00:55:09.850
And the ring finger's you gotta
make a commitment to Jesus.

00:55:09.850 --> 00:55:11.400
And then the pinky is
like, you got a little,

00:55:11.400 --> 00:55:13.090
now your faith begins to grow.

00:55:13.090 --> 00:55:13.923
It's a little faith.

00:55:13.923 --> 00:55:15.150
That's probably not what it is.

00:55:15.150 --> 00:55:18.022
It's basically taking
people down the Romans road.

00:55:18.022 --> 00:55:21.270
But you see these tools that we were given

00:55:21.270 --> 00:55:22.910
that then we had to put into practice

00:55:22.910 --> 00:55:24.200
and every night we would come back

00:55:24.200 --> 00:55:25.060
and we'd have these-

00:55:25.060 --> 00:55:26.650
Gospel in a hand is what we called it.

00:55:26.650 --> 00:55:29.993
Circles where we would do popcorn prayer.

00:55:32.500 --> 00:55:36.130
We would share our experiences
and pray for one another.

00:55:36.130 --> 00:55:37.967
And I was a seventh grader.

00:55:37.967 --> 00:55:39.770
It was very meaningful because we didn't,

00:55:39.770 --> 00:55:43.240
there wasn't a lot that we
had to care about that deeply.

00:55:43.240 --> 00:55:44.680
Again, I think that's one of the reasons-

00:55:44.680 --> 00:55:46.940
Taking huge risks with your friends.

00:55:46.940 --> 00:55:48.130
That's why religion is so effective

00:55:48.130 --> 00:55:52.930
is that there is this
deep longing for meaning.

00:55:52.930 --> 00:55:56.830
I mean, I'm sitting there
just whacking off every day

00:55:56.830 --> 00:55:57.933
and thinking is this all there is?

00:55:57.933 --> 00:55:59.180
It's just whacking off?

00:55:59.180 --> 00:56:01.410
I mean, it's okay, it's pretty cool.

00:56:01.410 --> 00:56:02.823
But you're feeling horrible about it.

00:56:02.823 --> 00:56:03.656
I was feeling horrible.

00:56:03.656 --> 00:56:05.030
I wasn't feeling as horrible about it

00:56:05.030 --> 00:56:08.960
as I would in high school,
it got worse and worse.

00:56:08.960 --> 00:56:10.943
But what I'm getting at, is I was like,

00:56:11.910 --> 00:56:15.200
there's something lasting
and there's something deeper

00:56:15.200 --> 00:56:17.610
and more meaningful, and
there's something eternal

00:56:17.610 --> 00:56:18.710
that I can be a part of.

00:56:18.710 --> 00:56:20.800
The work that we're doing is eternal.

00:56:20.800 --> 00:56:24.893
And we were, you and I,
there was a group of friends,

00:56:25.930 --> 00:56:29.380
but like there were more girls than guys

00:56:30.250 --> 00:56:31.930
in our youth group.
Holly, Holly was a part

00:56:31.930 --> 00:56:32.890
of all these trips.

00:56:32.890 --> 00:56:35.010
Well, you're making a point
that there were older girls,

00:56:35.010 --> 00:56:36.460
there were high school girls

00:56:37.788 --> 00:56:40.030
and a few guys on these trips too.

00:56:40.030 --> 00:56:40.863
But that was the other thing,

00:56:40.863 --> 00:56:44.240
is that you're sitting there praying for,

00:56:44.240 --> 00:56:47.340
and hearing this high school girl pray

00:56:47.340 --> 00:56:49.950
and maybe she'll pray for me
or maybe I can pray for her.

00:56:49.950 --> 00:56:50.940
It was intimate.

00:56:50.940 --> 00:56:53.160
It was like, it was awesome.

00:56:53.160 --> 00:56:56.930
But you and I were the most
committed of our friend group,

00:56:56.930 --> 00:56:59.200
and we were like the only guys

00:57:00.623 --> 00:57:03.220
who were as committed as we were.

00:57:03.220 --> 00:57:05.690
So it very much resonated with us

00:57:05.690 --> 00:57:10.690
and became even more of a
center point of our friendship.

00:57:11.260 --> 00:57:13.030
Right, to bring it back to us,

00:57:13.030 --> 00:57:15.300
the way that we were
connecting with each other.

00:57:15.300 --> 00:57:17.810
And I think this is
when a certain dynamic,

00:57:17.810 --> 00:57:20.670
from my perspective, this is
a dynamic that was developing.

00:57:20.670 --> 00:57:22.390
And I wanna hear what
you think about this.

00:57:22.390 --> 00:57:23.223
Okay.

00:57:26.660 --> 00:57:30.860
My parents were together,
still are together,

00:57:30.860 --> 00:57:35.860
and my dad, the way I saw
my dad was that he had...

00:57:37.250 --> 00:57:39.620
He had the knowledge
about everything, right?

00:57:39.620 --> 00:57:40.980
You wanna know what to
think about politics,

00:57:40.980 --> 00:57:43.970
you wanna know what to
think about religion,

00:57:43.970 --> 00:57:45.780
he's got it figured out.

00:57:45.780 --> 00:57:48.760
And he was smart, but he
was also very assertive

00:57:48.760 --> 00:57:49.860
in how he talked about it.

00:57:49.860 --> 00:57:51.617
Yeah.
And confident.

00:57:51.617 --> 00:57:55.730
And so I would sort of take
the things that he said,

00:57:55.730 --> 00:57:57.580
and yes, the things that
our youth pastor said,

00:57:57.580 --> 00:57:59.960
but it's just, you really
wanna know what's the real deal

00:57:59.960 --> 00:58:01.600
you gotta talk to my dad.

00:58:01.600 --> 00:58:04.260
And then I would sort of assimilate that

00:58:04.260 --> 00:58:08.440
into the Rhett version, which whatever.

00:58:08.440 --> 00:58:10.540
And then I would be like, well,

00:58:10.540 --> 00:58:13.150
I've gotta get Link
straightened out about,

00:58:13.150 --> 00:58:15.600
he's gotta know what to
think about politics.

00:58:15.600 --> 00:58:19.370
'Cause that relates to
your faith and the role

00:58:19.370 --> 00:58:23.180
and what God is trying to do
through politics in the world.

00:58:23.180 --> 00:58:27.630
And this is what you need to
think about predestination.

00:58:27.630 --> 00:58:29.750
This is what you need
to think about Baptism.

00:58:29.750 --> 00:58:32.900
I've got this information,
now I've gotta give it to you

00:58:32.900 --> 00:58:34.570
because well, you don't have a dad at home

00:58:34.570 --> 00:58:36.113
who's telling you these things.

00:58:37.451 --> 00:58:39.540
Now, this was not a calculated thing

00:58:39.540 --> 00:58:41.440
and I never thought about it that way,

00:58:41.440 --> 00:58:44.120
but that dynamic existed.

00:58:44.120 --> 00:58:45.350
Like I was getting this information

00:58:45.350 --> 00:58:50.340
and I had a willing recipient
of that information in you.

00:58:50.340 --> 00:58:54.380
Oh yeah, I mean, we did
talk about beliefs a lot

00:58:54.380 --> 00:58:59.380
and yes, whatever the dinner
table conversation was

00:59:00.550 --> 00:59:03.890
at your house about politics or religion,

00:59:03.890 --> 00:59:06.640
we would talk about the
next day and I would just,

00:59:06.640 --> 00:59:07.980
I'd be like, okay, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:59:07.980 --> 00:59:09.760
It's like, just, you know,

00:59:09.760 --> 00:59:12.240
I was just looking for youth pastor

00:59:12.240 --> 00:59:14.340
and I guess your dad through you

00:59:14.340 --> 00:59:18.450
to just tell me what to believe,
give me the right answers.

00:59:18.450 --> 00:59:21.403
I'll ace this test, you
know, this feels good.

00:59:22.850 --> 00:59:26.580
So yeah, that was my predisposition.

00:59:26.580 --> 00:59:28.800
And neither one of us-
So to kinda lock in.

00:59:28.800 --> 00:59:32.440
Neither one of us had a

00:59:34.130 --> 00:59:38.260
contrarian or questioning
aspect to our personality.

00:59:38.260 --> 00:59:41.400
Now, I kinda developed that
over time, I mean, obviously,

00:59:41.400 --> 00:59:45.680
by deconstructing and there's
a little bit of that in me

00:59:46.520 --> 00:59:47.780
that came out.

00:59:47.780 --> 00:59:49.303
But at the time it was-

00:59:49.303 --> 00:59:52.570
That wasn't until late
college and afterwards.

00:59:52.570 --> 00:59:54.190
There were kids who
were already being like,

00:59:54.190 --> 00:59:55.780
well, hold up now, really?

00:59:55.780 --> 00:59:57.850
This is what y'all,
this is what we believe?

00:59:57.850 --> 01:00:01.490
We were just primed for,
this is what we believe.

01:00:01.490 --> 01:00:04.240
Yes, how do I believe
it really, really hard?

01:00:04.240 --> 01:00:08.210
How do I align my life
completely with it in every way?

01:00:08.210 --> 01:00:11.060
It was like, this is the
most important thing.

01:00:11.060 --> 01:00:13.503
Never crossed my mind
that it might not be true.

01:00:14.450 --> 01:00:15.283
Yeah.
It was just like,

01:00:15.283 --> 01:00:16.960
this is it, man.

01:00:16.960 --> 01:00:20.570
And by you, what I was getting from you

01:00:21.560 --> 01:00:24.090
if I would bring something like that was,

01:00:24.090 --> 01:00:28.070
oh, he's just as serious
about this as I am.

01:00:28.070 --> 01:00:31.860
And so we began to separate a little bit,

01:00:31.860 --> 01:00:33.690
especially from our guy friends.

01:00:33.690 --> 01:00:36.540
Right.
As these guys who we would,

01:00:36.540 --> 01:00:38.513
we took this seriously.

01:00:39.440 --> 01:00:42.950
We began to bond more aggressively

01:00:42.950 --> 01:00:44.870
because of the way that we were separating

01:00:44.870 --> 01:00:49.300
and kind of gravitating
towards this view of our faith.

01:00:49.300 --> 01:00:53.030
Yeah, and seventh grade was the last year

01:00:53.030 --> 01:00:56.880
that Ben was in school
before he went to homeschool.

01:00:56.880 --> 01:00:57.780
I think.
That was another big factor.

01:00:57.780 --> 01:01:00.520
He was easing out into homeschool

01:01:00.520 --> 01:01:02.500
because of his chronic fatigue syndrome,

01:01:02.500 --> 01:01:05.300
and so by eighth grade he
was basically not there.

01:01:05.300 --> 01:01:08.993
He didn't go to church with
us, but we would still,

01:01:11.330 --> 01:01:13.320
he still participated in
the talent show with us.

01:01:13.320 --> 01:01:16.570
We'd still go to his house
and like do dance routines

01:01:16.570 --> 01:01:18.300
and learn like rap songs and stuff.

01:01:18.300 --> 01:01:20.260
So, I mean, y'all were still very tight.

01:01:20.260 --> 01:01:21.160
But the amount of time-

01:01:21.160 --> 01:01:24.100
It was this point where we
became more of a threesome

01:01:25.530 --> 01:01:26.622
Well, I would say,
Friendship.

01:01:26.622 --> 01:01:29.090
I think we were more
of a threesome earlier,

01:01:29.090 --> 01:01:32.970
and then I think this is when,
with Ben's chronic fatigue,

01:01:32.970 --> 01:01:35.820
there were days when I
would call over there

01:01:35.820 --> 01:01:38.943
and Ms. Greenwood would answer
the phone and she'd be like,

01:01:38.943 --> 01:01:41.080
"Ben can't do anything right now."

01:01:41.080 --> 01:01:41.913
Yeah.

01:01:41.913 --> 01:01:43.223
And as a seventh grader,

01:01:45.380 --> 01:01:46.970
especially me, I just was not,

01:01:46.970 --> 01:01:49.700
I've never been, I'm
still having problems...

01:01:50.760 --> 01:01:51.676
Right.

01:01:51.676 --> 01:01:53.220
Talking to people.

01:01:53.220 --> 01:01:55.540
Which we've already talked about that.

01:01:55.540 --> 01:01:57.430
So that ended up being this thing where,

01:01:57.430 --> 01:01:59.390
okay, I'm not spending this time with Ben,

01:01:59.390 --> 01:02:00.360
I'm not doing this thing.

01:02:00.360 --> 01:02:01.540
Every day during the summer

01:02:01.540 --> 01:02:04.040
between fifth grade and sixth grade,

01:02:04.040 --> 01:02:05.980
I was gonna be at Ben's
house doing something

01:02:05.980 --> 01:02:07.830
and you would be there a lot.

01:02:07.830 --> 01:02:09.840
But the default was,

01:02:09.840 --> 01:02:11.340
we're going to Keith
Hills to do something.

01:02:11.340 --> 01:02:13.160
And that was changing.

01:02:13.160 --> 01:02:16.590
Now it became, this is when
in the "Book of Mythicality",

01:02:16.590 --> 01:02:17.630
we're talking about you and I

01:02:17.630 --> 01:02:18.740
Riding the bikes.
riding our bikes,

01:02:18.740 --> 01:02:21.920
and meeting at the midpoint
at the middle of town.

01:02:21.920 --> 01:02:23.990
Yeah.
And I had enough confidence

01:02:23.990 --> 01:02:24.840
as a seventh grader

01:02:24.840 --> 01:02:26.070
to start riding my bike everywhere

01:02:26.070 --> 01:02:28.390
and spending the night
at your house more often.

01:02:28.390 --> 01:02:31.680
And that's when we would start
doing the flashlight wars

01:02:31.680 --> 01:02:34.850
and we would go to the Keith Hills gate.

01:02:34.850 --> 01:02:36.730
I would come along for the
things that you'd been doing

01:02:36.730 --> 01:02:39.253
for years with your neighborhood friends.

01:02:41.330 --> 01:02:42.840
Setting off fireworks at the gate

01:02:42.840 --> 01:02:45.260
and having the security guards show up,

01:02:45.260 --> 01:02:48.170
or like the campus police
for Campbell University.

01:02:48.170 --> 01:02:50.527
And they would get on
the loud speaker and say,

01:02:50.527 --> 01:02:53.700
"Boys, we know you're
in the woods, come out."

01:02:53.700 --> 01:02:54.920
I loved it.
And then I would start

01:02:54.920 --> 01:02:57.250
to come out and realize I
was the only one walking out

01:02:57.250 --> 01:02:58.760
and then I wouldn't walk out.

01:02:58.760 --> 01:03:01.510
And then we would sprint
back to your house,

01:03:01.510 --> 01:03:06.440
and I was so much slower that
me and your neighbor, Heather,

01:03:06.440 --> 01:03:07.740
weren't fast enough to keep up,

01:03:07.740 --> 01:03:11.560
and we got caught in the
headlights of the security car.

01:03:11.560 --> 01:03:15.400
And then we had to go talk
to the security guard.

01:03:15.400 --> 01:03:18.573
But he gave us a really
strong lecture and let us go

01:03:18.573 --> 01:03:19.670
'cause you ditched us!

01:03:19.670 --> 01:03:23.740
Yeah, man, I mean, every man for himself.

01:03:23.740 --> 01:03:25.950
So yeah, I was kinda
getting in the mix there

01:03:25.950 --> 01:03:28.890
and having more sleepovers,

01:03:28.890 --> 01:03:31.407
and starting to gain confidence.

01:03:31.407 --> 01:03:34.060
And none of that was
registering with me by the way.

01:03:34.060 --> 01:03:36.380
Things didn't register, I
don't think things registered.

01:03:36.380 --> 01:03:38.500
Things registered with me in terms of,

01:03:38.500 --> 01:03:41.793
'cause I remember basketball
was the another thing where,

01:03:42.740 --> 01:03:46.111
I mean, we were definitely
best friends by this point.

01:03:46.111 --> 01:03:47.900
And church was the engine

01:03:47.900 --> 01:03:50.850
and then school was
just icing on the cake.

01:03:50.850 --> 01:03:55.300
But like you were on the
basketball team as a seventh grader

01:03:55.300 --> 01:03:56.823
and eighth grader, of course.

01:03:58.830 --> 01:04:00.900
I find it wild that we
did play soccer together.

01:04:00.900 --> 01:04:02.150
That's something that I tend to forget,

01:04:02.150 --> 01:04:03.400
I wanna come back to the basketball,

01:04:03.400 --> 01:04:05.470
but like you were the goalie, right?

01:04:05.470 --> 01:04:08.809
Yeah, much to my, I did not like it.

01:04:08.809 --> 01:04:10.121
They're like, you're so big.
'Cause you were so tall.

01:04:10.121 --> 01:04:13.122
I was like, man, I should be
up there scoring goals, man.

01:04:13.122 --> 01:04:15.640
And I played, I liked soccer.

01:04:15.640 --> 01:04:18.580
I was pretty good at it, at defense.

01:04:18.580 --> 01:04:20.640
You know, I couldn't run a lot,

01:04:20.640 --> 01:04:21.930
I didn't have a lot of stamina,

01:04:21.930 --> 01:04:24.180
hence the security guard thing.

01:04:24.180 --> 01:04:27.290
But I could play some good defense.

01:04:27.290 --> 01:04:28.470
Yeah, 'cause sports were becoming

01:04:28.470 --> 01:04:29.750
a really big part of my life.

01:04:29.750 --> 01:04:30.977
And, of course, I was
gonna play everything

01:04:30.977 --> 01:04:32.750
that we were allowed to play,

01:04:32.750 --> 01:04:34.710
which those were the three sports,

01:04:34.710 --> 01:04:36.030
soccer, baseball and basketball.

01:04:36.030 --> 01:04:38.200
And I was just hanging
on by the soccer thread.

01:04:38.200 --> 01:04:39.033
Other than that-

01:04:39.033 --> 01:04:41.320
And again, I didn't know that you,

01:04:41.320 --> 01:04:43.180
'cause we didn't talk about these things.

01:04:43.180 --> 01:04:45.930
Now, as a parent of teenage boys,

01:04:45.930 --> 01:04:47.730
I get the fact that you're just not,

01:04:47.730 --> 01:04:48.940
there's a whole part of your brain

01:04:48.940 --> 01:04:50.450
that you're not really using

01:04:50.450 --> 01:04:52.770
and you really look back on
these things reflectively.

01:04:52.770 --> 01:04:56.853
But I'm thinking of things
like, he's playing basketball,

01:04:57.710 --> 01:04:59.820
and he practices basketball so much,

01:04:59.820 --> 01:05:03.060
this really infringes on
us being able to hang out.

01:05:03.060 --> 01:05:06.760
I believe, I'm pretty positive,

01:05:06.760 --> 01:05:09.860
that the reason why I
started keeping score

01:05:09.860 --> 01:05:12.020
for the girls basketball team

01:05:12.020 --> 01:05:16.060
was because it gave me something
to do that I was attached,

01:05:16.060 --> 01:05:19.700
I traveled with the basketball
team with the boys and girls.

01:05:19.700 --> 01:05:21.180
So I could-
You get to go on a bus.

01:05:21.180 --> 01:05:22.900
I got to go on the bus

01:05:22.900 --> 01:05:25.480
and I could keep score for the girls

01:05:25.480 --> 01:05:27.820
and then just watch the boys game.

01:05:27.820 --> 01:05:28.653
'Cause I thought it was cooler

01:05:28.653 --> 01:05:30.910
to be able to watch the boys game.

01:05:30.910 --> 01:05:31.900
Versus keep up with it.

01:05:31.900 --> 01:05:33.320
Yeah.

01:05:33.320 --> 01:05:34.980
Pandora though, she was
tough to keep up with.

01:05:34.980 --> 01:05:36.060
She was tough to keep up with

01:05:36.060 --> 01:05:38.080
as I recall.
She was a stat machine.

01:05:38.080 --> 01:05:40.540
Yeah, just a layup tyrant.

01:05:40.540 --> 01:05:44.290
But that was part of my equation.

01:05:44.290 --> 01:05:46.951
It's like, I wanna be in the mix, I wanna,

01:05:46.951 --> 01:05:49.320
what's my middle school identity?

01:05:49.320 --> 01:05:52.680
I'm okay with being the
scorekeeper if I can be on the bus,

01:05:52.680 --> 01:05:54.070
if I can be in the mix.

01:05:54.070 --> 01:05:57.290
And you were, so you were
processing things on that level?

01:05:57.290 --> 01:05:58.723
I remember that, yeah.

01:05:59.860 --> 01:06:01.200
Yeah, to me-

01:06:01.200 --> 01:06:03.850
But I also knew that I
would never talk about it

01:06:03.850 --> 01:06:06.170
'cause it's like talking
about your feelings

01:06:06.170 --> 01:06:09.450
and being needy is just
a real sign of weakness.

01:06:09.450 --> 01:06:11.073
Like what am I supposed to say?

01:06:11.073 --> 01:06:16.073
It's like, well, do you
think I can be a scorekeeper

01:06:17.020 --> 01:06:19.140
so that we can spend time together?

01:06:19.140 --> 01:06:24.140
But just to clarify, our
kids talk about that stuff.

01:06:25.650 --> 01:06:28.130
It is very much a generational

01:06:28.130 --> 01:06:29.650
and also a geographical thing.

01:06:29.650 --> 01:06:31.280
There was probably kids in certain places

01:06:31.280 --> 01:06:33.117
in the world that were,

01:06:33.117 --> 01:06:35.710
and in our community that just wasn't

01:06:35.710 --> 01:06:38.690
how you process life as a kid.

01:06:38.690 --> 01:06:40.390
Right.
So for me, it was just like,

01:06:40.390 --> 01:06:42.220
oh, there's a sport, I can play it.

01:06:42.220 --> 01:06:44.020
I can be good at it, okay.

01:06:44.020 --> 01:06:47.910
It never crossed my mind do I want?

01:06:47.910 --> 01:06:49.330
Am I having fun?

01:06:49.330 --> 01:06:51.680
It wasn't about having
fun, it was about winning.

01:06:51.680 --> 01:06:52.880
It never crossed my mind.

01:06:52.880 --> 01:06:53.930
It was just, you have to be as good

01:06:53.930 --> 01:06:55.840
as you possibly can at this.

01:06:55.840 --> 01:06:56.894
And of course you have to be as good

01:06:56.894 --> 01:06:58.307
as you possibly can at this,

01:06:58.307 --> 01:06:59.779
and you have to be as good
as you possibly can at this.

01:06:59.779 --> 01:07:00.719
And it wasn't just sports.

01:07:00.719 --> 01:07:01.867
It was, oh, you also have to be

01:07:01.867 --> 01:07:04.080
as good as you possibly can at academics.

01:07:04.080 --> 01:07:05.310
You have to be as good as you possibly can

01:07:05.310 --> 01:07:08.653
at the talent show, at giving
a speech for the class.

01:07:09.610 --> 01:07:11.990
But it didn't feel like pressure.

01:07:11.990 --> 01:07:14.250
It just felt like a way of being.

01:07:14.250 --> 01:07:15.760
Yeah, this is the plan.

01:07:15.760 --> 01:07:18.890
I mean, and it was such
a tight-knit community.

01:07:18.890 --> 01:07:23.890
In our middle school classes,
seventh and eighth grade,

01:07:24.810 --> 01:07:26.190
we had the same three teachers;

01:07:26.190 --> 01:07:28.780
Ms. Wakus, Ms. McClam and Mr. Royal,

01:07:28.780 --> 01:07:31.983
who was also the basketball
coach, PE coach, health teacher.

01:07:36.530 --> 01:07:40.210
Our group of friends, whenever
something would happen

01:07:40.210 --> 01:07:41.641
or there'd be like strife,

01:07:41.641 --> 01:07:44.390
there will be drama with the girls

01:07:44.390 --> 01:07:46.890
and sometimes it would
involve guys and dating,

01:07:46.890 --> 01:07:51.590
and sometimes it would be
us doing like stupid stuff.

01:07:51.590 --> 01:07:54.120
But regardless of what happened,

01:07:54.120 --> 01:07:57.100
that there was drama or trouble,

01:07:57.100 --> 01:08:00.400
all three teachers would
take it upon themselves

01:08:00.400 --> 01:08:03.000
to hold a tribunal to resolve it.

01:08:03.000 --> 01:08:06.340
They wouldn't send anybody
to the principal's office.

01:08:06.340 --> 01:08:08.930
They were sparing him 'cause
he had the rest of the school

01:08:08.930 --> 01:08:10.470
and I think that they liked to be

01:08:10.470 --> 01:08:13.510
more heavy-handed than he was.

01:08:13.510 --> 01:08:17.110
You remember, you'd be in
one class and all of a sudden

01:08:18.330 --> 01:08:20.210
a knock on the door and another teacher

01:08:20.210 --> 01:08:22.610
would call the teacher
out and then we just,

01:08:22.610 --> 01:08:24.380
we'll be back in a minute student.

01:08:24.380 --> 01:08:27.617
And then instead of coming
back in, she would say,

01:08:27.617 --> 01:08:31.080
"Rhett, Link, can you
step out into the hall?"

01:08:31.080 --> 01:08:33.180
And then we'd step out into the hall,

01:08:33.180 --> 01:08:38.090
and all three teachers would
be there laying into us,

01:08:38.090 --> 01:08:41.170
questioning us about something we did.

01:08:41.170 --> 01:08:43.890
And sometimes it would
be like calling the girls

01:08:43.890 --> 01:08:45.710
out into the hall and
there'd be like six girls

01:08:45.710 --> 01:08:47.910
out in the hall with the three teachers

01:08:47.910 --> 01:08:52.410
just trying to have a,

01:08:52.410 --> 01:08:54.920
it's a tribunal, they were put on trial.

01:08:54.920 --> 01:08:56.760
And it was kind of amazing!

01:08:56.760 --> 01:08:58.930
I remember this in general.

01:08:58.930 --> 01:09:00.740
I don't remember it as
specifically as you do.

01:09:00.740 --> 01:09:02.610
I knew I remember when
the six girls would go,

01:09:02.610 --> 01:09:04.380
okay, well I know why
these are the six girls

01:09:04.380 --> 01:09:06.630
who are going out because
they had a sleepover,

01:09:06.630 --> 01:09:08.680
but that one girl wasn't invited

01:09:08.680 --> 01:09:11.180
and now her feelings are hurt.

01:09:11.180 --> 01:09:14.870
So that level of drama,
that middle school drama.

01:09:14.870 --> 01:09:17.680
The teachers were, one of 'em was a parent

01:09:17.680 --> 01:09:19.000
of one of our friends.

01:09:19.000 --> 01:09:20.500
Right, one of the girls
in the group, yeah.

01:09:20.500 --> 01:09:23.513
But they were all like very involved.

01:09:23.513 --> 01:09:24.886
I never thought about that being

01:09:24.886 --> 01:09:26.783
one of the dynamics.
It was like street justice.

01:09:26.783 --> 01:09:27.950
Yeah.
I don't remember

01:09:27.950 --> 01:09:29.680
what the punishments were?

01:09:29.680 --> 01:09:31.860
We never really got much of a-

01:09:31.860 --> 01:09:33.230
It wasn't really punishments

01:09:33.230 --> 01:09:35.810
as much as it was the
tribunal and the lecture

01:09:35.810 --> 01:09:36.893
was the punishment.

01:09:37.920 --> 01:09:38.753
You know?
Yeah.

01:09:38.753 --> 01:09:40.770
'Cause we never got suspended.

01:09:40.770 --> 01:09:43.670
No, because everything we were doing,

01:09:43.670 --> 01:09:46.480
we were all really, we all
made really good grades.

01:09:46.480 --> 01:09:48.146
And we were good kids,
We were good students.

01:09:48.146 --> 01:09:49.310
but we were mischievous.

01:09:49.310 --> 01:09:52.140
Yeah, which we definitely
carried on in high school.

01:09:52.140 --> 01:09:54.450
But that middle school drama thing,

01:09:54.450 --> 01:09:56.340
they were, it's just so fascinating

01:09:56.340 --> 01:10:00.000
that they were so in the weeds,
and we were scared of 'em.

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:02.180
And that's when I was told,

01:10:02.180 --> 01:10:04.880
when you get to high school next year,

01:10:04.880 --> 01:10:07.900
they're gonna stuff you in a locker.

01:10:07.900 --> 01:10:11.020
Well, as it relates to me and you,

01:10:11.020 --> 01:10:12.750
I mean, I'm surprised that
that didn't happen, but as

01:10:12.750 --> 01:10:14.590
it relates to me and you,
Never happened.

01:10:14.590 --> 01:10:18.280
I think that middle school
is when people began

01:10:18.280 --> 01:10:20.320
to think of us as a duo.

01:10:20.320 --> 01:10:21.153
Yeah.

01:10:21.153 --> 01:10:22.710
Right?
Yeah.

01:10:22.710 --> 01:10:24.090
The teachers.
Yep.

01:10:24.090 --> 01:10:24.953
That's why if you got
called into something,

01:10:24.953 --> 01:10:27.183
I would get called and vice versa.

01:10:28.300 --> 01:10:32.220
Our friends, it's like, oh,
Rhett and Link, they're a duo.

01:10:32.220 --> 01:10:36.810
And again, this was not
like an intentional thing,

01:10:36.810 --> 01:10:39.820
but it was like, oh,
there's a school dance,

01:10:39.820 --> 01:10:41.230
there's the lip sync contest.

01:10:41.230 --> 01:10:42.760
It was like, oh, well we're gonna do

01:10:42.760 --> 01:10:44.330
DJ Jazzy Jeff &amp; The Fresh Prince

01:10:44.330 --> 01:10:47.920
because they're the ultimate
duo, we're the ultimate duo.

01:10:47.920 --> 01:10:49.620
We started performing together,

01:10:49.620 --> 01:10:51.400
we started seeing ourselves as a team.

01:10:51.400 --> 01:10:53.900
If we could work together on a project,

01:10:53.900 --> 01:10:55.230
if there was a way to be like,

01:10:55.230 --> 01:10:56.787
hey, we're gonna do this Oedipus video.

01:10:56.787 --> 01:11:00.350
That's right and we used
Anna's video camera and we did

01:11:00.350 --> 01:11:01.690
our first video project.
And it was like

01:11:01.690 --> 01:11:03.850
we were kind of thinking
about it in that way,

01:11:03.850 --> 01:11:05.540
and then we did a whole different one

01:11:05.540 --> 01:11:07.460
over at Heather's house.

01:11:07.460 --> 01:11:09.180
Yeah.
The next year.

01:11:09.180 --> 01:11:11.250
There was a cornfield and a scarecrow.

01:11:11.250 --> 01:11:12.770
Yeah.

01:11:12.770 --> 01:11:17.200
But we just naturally fell
into this creative partnership.

01:11:17.200 --> 01:11:18.660
That's right.

01:11:18.660 --> 01:11:21.680
That really hadn't happened
up until that point, right?

01:11:21.680 --> 01:11:23.330
It happened through the talent shows,

01:11:23.330 --> 01:11:25.160
through video projects,

01:11:25.160 --> 01:11:28.360
that was when we began to
work together creatively.

01:11:28.360 --> 01:11:31.270
And I think that, now
we had done things like

01:11:31.270 --> 01:11:34.420
the stupid little radio shows
and stuff at the house, right?

01:11:34.420 --> 01:11:35.700
Yeah, the pause tapes.

01:11:35.700 --> 01:11:37.960
But what I found and, of
course, I'm going around,

01:11:37.960 --> 01:11:39.750
I'm sampling every friendship.

01:11:39.750 --> 01:11:41.190
You know what I was doing,

01:11:41.190 --> 01:11:42.650
I was going to every single house,

01:11:42.650 --> 01:11:44.670
I was spending the night
at everybody's house.

01:11:44.670 --> 01:11:48.170
And I had in mind, not an agenda,

01:11:48.170 --> 01:11:51.220
but I sort of just naturally
carried with me an agenda

01:11:51.220 --> 01:11:53.296
without thinking about it.

01:11:53.296 --> 01:11:54.520
It was just my personality.

01:11:54.520 --> 01:11:55.900
To what?

01:11:55.900 --> 01:11:57.290
I wanna have fun in a particular way.

01:11:57.290 --> 01:11:58.870
Okay.
I am interested

01:11:58.870 --> 01:12:00.580
in having fun in this way.

01:12:00.580 --> 01:12:02.270
Okay, now we've got this radio.

01:12:02.270 --> 01:12:04.580
I think that we should do this radio show

01:12:04.580 --> 01:12:05.650
and we should do characters,

01:12:05.650 --> 01:12:08.300
we should do voices and that kinda thing.

01:12:08.300 --> 01:12:10.913
There was no one else who
was receptive to that,

01:12:12.090 --> 01:12:13.900
besides you.

01:12:13.900 --> 01:12:15.070
I was all in.

01:12:15.070 --> 01:12:15.903
You know what I'm saying?

01:12:15.903 --> 01:12:17.760
No one was receptive
to that way of thinking

01:12:17.760 --> 01:12:20.450
about amusing themselves.

01:12:20.450 --> 01:12:22.453
Ben was! Ben was.

01:12:23.410 --> 01:12:27.240
Well, as we discussed, Ben
was even more of an ideas guy.

01:12:27.240 --> 01:12:29.430
Yeah, right.
And he was more out there.

01:12:29.430 --> 01:12:32.607
I mean, again, when you go over
to his house and he's like,

01:12:32.607 --> 01:12:34.793
"Let me show you the new
dance that I invented",

01:12:34.793 --> 01:12:35.690
and it's kinda

01:12:35.690 --> 01:12:37.360
like "Riverdance".

01:12:37.360 --> 01:12:40.200
I mean, he was a wild
card, he was amazing.

01:12:40.200 --> 01:12:43.410
But yeah, I was very much like, yes!

01:12:43.410 --> 01:12:45.330
I had a sense of...

01:12:47.130 --> 01:12:49.470
Yeah, we're gonna hang out,
we're gonna make stuff,

01:12:49.470 --> 01:12:50.303
we're gonna do stuff.

01:12:50.303 --> 01:12:52.500
This is it, this is it, this is amazing.

01:12:52.500 --> 01:12:53.713
And regardless of what it was-

01:12:53.713 --> 01:12:56.013
This is precursor to the blood oath thing.

01:12:56.897 --> 01:12:58.351
I definitely had that sense

01:12:58.351 --> 01:12:59.820
at that point.
If we're gonna hang out

01:12:59.820 --> 01:13:03.210
for an afternoon,
whether it was me and you

01:13:03.210 --> 01:13:06.810
or Ben was there or not, it might be like,

01:13:06.810 --> 01:13:08.260
all right, we're not
just getting together,

01:13:08.260 --> 01:13:10.540
we're getting together
and today's mission is,

01:13:10.540 --> 01:13:11.750
fill in the blank.

01:13:11.750 --> 01:13:13.393
Go out and cut down a tree.

01:13:14.780 --> 01:13:16.870
Try to make a raft that we're gonna float

01:13:16.870 --> 01:13:17.703
in the backyard.

01:13:17.703 --> 01:13:18.870
Take some stuff off of this tree

01:13:18.870 --> 01:13:21.240
and to make a tea that
Ben said we should make,

01:13:21.240 --> 01:13:23.980
because we found it in
a book in the library.

01:13:23.980 --> 01:13:24.813
Yeah.

01:13:24.813 --> 01:13:26.530
We had missions, we had agendas.

01:13:26.530 --> 01:13:28.030
Go into that sewer!

01:13:28.030 --> 01:13:31.110
Right, it carried with
it a sense of mission.

01:13:31.110 --> 01:13:32.520
And then that was,

01:13:32.520 --> 01:13:34.810
we filled our time with
that way of thinking,

01:13:34.810 --> 01:13:38.770
and then we also filmed
any creative endeavor

01:13:38.770 --> 01:13:40.540
with the same sense of mission.

01:13:40.540 --> 01:13:42.620
Like this is gonna be
good and this has gotta be

01:13:42.620 --> 01:13:44.070
the best thing that we've ever done.

01:13:44.070 --> 01:13:45.780
Again, we never talked about it.

01:13:45.780 --> 01:13:47.627
There was no, let's have a
meeting and figure this out.

01:13:47.627 --> 01:13:50.683
It was in a natural thing where,

01:13:51.830 --> 01:13:53.113
and then with Ben sort of checking out

01:13:53.113 --> 01:13:56.120
because of the health
problems that he was having.

01:13:56.120 --> 01:13:58.400
I mean, I still believe to this day

01:13:58.400 --> 01:14:02.130
that if Ben had not had those issues,

01:14:02.130 --> 01:14:03.130
the three of us would've grown up

01:14:03.130 --> 01:14:04.410
and started something together.

01:14:04.410 --> 01:14:07.160
Yeah, I believe that.
Something creative together.

01:14:08.038 --> 01:14:10.131
But what ended up happening-

01:14:10.131 --> 01:14:11.650
It was a sense of identity.

01:14:11.650 --> 01:14:13.778
The two of us were kinda left.

01:14:13.778 --> 01:14:16.830
And then people started
to group us together.

01:14:16.830 --> 01:14:19.450
That's when people would say,
it would be Rhett and Link,

01:14:19.450 --> 01:14:22.040
Rhett and Link this, that, and the other.

01:14:22.040 --> 01:14:23.720
A lot of weekends we would hang out,

01:14:23.720 --> 01:14:25.183
we'd spend the night at one
person's house or the other,

01:14:25.183 --> 01:14:29.700
I think it started to happen
a whole lot at this phase.

01:14:29.700 --> 01:14:34.700
And so, it was this identity
of, oh, these guys are a duo.

01:14:35.830 --> 01:14:39.170
Yeah, I definitely think that
people thought of it that way.

01:14:39.170 --> 01:14:40.003
Yeah.

01:14:40.003 --> 01:14:41.330
And there's another dynamic

01:14:41.330 --> 01:14:43.230
that started to develop at this point.

01:14:46.580 --> 01:14:48.730
That I think is a dynamic
that has characterized

01:14:48.730 --> 01:14:50.780
a lot of our friendship since that point.

01:14:52.590 --> 01:14:57.530
And we wrote this into "The
Lost Causes of Bleak Creek"

01:14:57.530 --> 01:14:59.870
in the way that we
described the Rex character

01:14:59.870 --> 01:15:02.430
talking to the leaf
character about something,

01:15:02.430 --> 01:15:03.830
about the film school thing.

01:15:05.601 --> 01:15:08.360
And I remember, the
first time I can remember

01:15:08.360 --> 01:15:10.890
that this happened was it
was between eighth grade

01:15:10.890 --> 01:15:13.770
and ninth grade, so we
graduated from eighth grade

01:15:13.770 --> 01:15:16.790
and we were getting ready
to go to high school.

01:15:16.790 --> 01:15:19.190
We were swimming in the
river like we did so often,

01:15:19.190 --> 01:15:20.510
and so often that summer

01:15:20.510 --> 01:15:23.280
it was just me and you
swimming in the river, no Ben.

01:15:23.280 --> 01:15:24.113
Right.

01:15:26.990 --> 01:15:30.209
I was casting vision.

01:15:30.209 --> 01:15:31.042
Right?

01:15:31.042 --> 01:15:31.875
For high school.

01:15:31.875 --> 01:15:32.708
For high school.

01:15:32.708 --> 01:15:35.400
This is what high school's
gonna be, get ready.

01:15:35.400 --> 01:15:38.350
And I was doing it through
the lens of the women

01:15:38.350 --> 01:15:39.700
that we were going to get to meet

01:15:39.700 --> 01:15:42.450
because now it wasn't just all the girls

01:15:42.450 --> 01:15:44.120
that we've known for eight years.

01:15:44.120 --> 01:15:45.253
It was the girls from Lillington

01:15:45.253 --> 01:15:48.450
and the girls from Anger.
The girls that we've seen

01:15:48.450 --> 01:15:49.660
And the girls from Lafayette.

01:15:49.660 --> 01:15:52.270
when you played 'em in basketball.

01:15:52.270 --> 01:15:53.880
The cheerleaders.
You saw the cheerleaders

01:15:53.880 --> 01:15:55.700
and you saw the girls in the stands

01:15:55.700 --> 01:15:59.280
and you saw, and hey, I met
a few score-keeping girls

01:15:59.280 --> 01:16:00.710
Right.
from other schools.

01:16:00.710 --> 01:16:02.900
Smarty pants with glasses on.

01:16:02.900 --> 01:16:05.220
Now-
Good with a pencil.

01:16:05.220 --> 01:16:10.040
And so, I remember sort of
trying to cast this vision,

01:16:10.040 --> 01:16:12.423
and again, that was when it kind of began.

01:16:13.381 --> 01:16:18.381
And then I feel like, I kinda
carried this role forward.

01:16:21.280 --> 01:16:22.113
Oh yeah.
To be like,

01:16:22.113 --> 01:16:23.820
I remember the specifics.
here's the vision

01:16:23.820 --> 01:16:26.370
and this is the way you
should be thinking about it.

01:16:26.370 --> 01:16:28.270
This is how you should be
getting excited about it.

01:16:28.270 --> 01:16:32.164
And again, you were this
incredibly receptive person

01:16:32.164 --> 01:16:34.680
that responded in a way

01:16:34.680 --> 01:16:36.760
that no one else would respond to me.

01:16:36.760 --> 01:16:38.490
Yeah.

01:16:38.490 --> 01:16:40.520
No one else would listen
to me in the same way.

01:16:40.520 --> 01:16:44.400
Yeah, and I was like, I
didn't just soak it up,

01:16:44.400 --> 01:16:45.480
I did give it back.

01:16:45.480 --> 01:16:47.510
It was this like, okay.

01:16:47.510 --> 01:16:48.343
You were on board.

01:16:48.343 --> 01:16:52.380
You were like, you know
that cheerleader from Anger,

01:16:52.380 --> 01:16:55.150
Melissa, like, oh man, she's-

01:16:55.150 --> 01:16:57.290
Yeah, she's at the top of the pyramid.

01:16:57.290 --> 01:16:59.361
She's, yeah.
Mm-hm.

01:16:59.361 --> 01:17:02.910
And then, I remember we
were talking about her,

01:17:02.910 --> 01:17:04.140
I remember two other girls

01:17:04.140 --> 01:17:06.090
we were talking about, specifically.

01:17:06.090 --> 01:17:08.120
They were both in relationships.

01:17:08.120 --> 01:17:09.440
That didn't matter.
It didn't matter.

01:17:09.440 --> 01:17:12.068
I said those would not
last over the summer.

01:17:12.068 --> 01:17:12.901
Right.

01:17:12.901 --> 01:17:14.068
I was right.

01:17:16.240 --> 01:17:18.040
Melissa stayed in a relationship,

01:17:18.040 --> 01:17:19.660
that did not pan out for me.

01:17:19.660 --> 01:17:20.493
Well-

01:17:20.493 --> 01:17:22.430
'Cause your vision, I think my vision too,

01:17:22.430 --> 01:17:25.310
was that I was gonna date her.

01:17:25.310 --> 01:17:27.173
We had two girls that we
were gonna date together.

01:17:27.173 --> 01:17:28.006
Yeah.

01:17:28.006 --> 01:17:30.270
Mine didn't work just because
she wasn't receptive to me,

01:17:30.270 --> 01:17:32.333
because I couldn't convince everyone

01:17:32.333 --> 01:17:34.380
of everything at all times.

01:17:34.380 --> 01:17:35.251
Right.

01:17:35.251 --> 01:17:38.610
Once they saw my face ,

01:17:38.610 --> 01:17:40.450
they'd be like, okay, well
you better be something,

01:17:40.450 --> 01:17:41.990
there better be some
other things going on.

01:17:41.990 --> 01:17:43.210
Stephanie's a smart girl.

01:17:43.210 --> 01:17:44.570
Yeah, right.
We've already said

01:17:44.570 --> 01:17:47.243
these names, so it might
as well go on record.

01:17:49.430 --> 01:17:53.220
But yeah, I do think that
that's when it really,

01:17:53.220 --> 01:17:55.560
when it really started to come together.

01:17:55.560 --> 01:17:58.040
You talk about the
accelerant of a mission trip,

01:17:58.040 --> 01:18:01.635
and we just took that
mentality into our friendship.

01:18:01.635 --> 01:18:04.380
Like we totally got each other

01:18:04.380 --> 01:18:06.473
and we were going in the same direction.

01:18:07.380 --> 01:18:11.410
We wanted the same things,
a lot of it being attention.

01:18:11.410 --> 01:18:14.060
And I was scared as hell of girls,

01:18:14.060 --> 01:18:19.060
but every day I was getting
more ready to overcome that.

01:18:20.770 --> 01:18:23.160
It would take until 10th grade to do so,

01:18:23.160 --> 01:18:26.170
but we can talk about that next time.

01:18:26.170 --> 01:18:28.110
'Cause I think this is
a good place to stop.

01:18:28.110 --> 01:18:29.530
The next time we pick up this series,

01:18:29.530 --> 01:18:31.410
of course, we'll be in high school,

01:18:31.410 --> 01:18:35.780
and that's an interesting
phase because our friendship,

01:18:35.780 --> 01:18:39.466
our friend group grew a lot.

01:18:39.466 --> 01:18:43.843
And then the things that we
were devoting our time to,

01:18:44.700 --> 01:18:48.060
you no longer played soccer and
I didn't have anything to do

01:18:48.060 --> 01:18:49.660
with keeping score for basketball.

01:18:49.660 --> 01:18:54.660
There was more, I'll say
threats to our closeness

01:18:56.470 --> 01:18:57.470
in a lot of ways.

01:18:57.470 --> 01:19:00.720
But then there were some key
things that counteracted that.

01:19:00.720 --> 01:19:02.720
So we can walk through all that

01:19:02.720 --> 01:19:05.680
and see what we can remember
next time we pick this up in,

01:19:05.680 --> 01:19:08.180
I don't know, in a month and a half.

01:19:08.180 --> 01:19:10.460
We'll keep going at
like that cadence, but-

01:19:10.460 --> 01:19:11.293
Okay.

01:19:12.550 --> 01:19:15.150
Yeah, middle school was big for us, man.

01:19:15.150 --> 01:19:17.563
So I mean, I think that
the thing that I had not,

01:19:18.550 --> 01:19:22.830
the thing I wanna remember is
the thing that I had forgotten

01:19:22.830 --> 01:19:25.260
until talking about it, which
I think that middle school

01:19:25.260 --> 01:19:27.870
is the beginning of the duo.

01:19:27.870 --> 01:19:28.860
Yeah.

01:19:28.860 --> 01:19:32.293
That was the beginning of
us being seen as a duo.

01:19:33.310 --> 01:19:34.900
Right.
That other people

01:19:34.900 --> 01:19:36.710
would have perceived us in that way.

01:19:36.710 --> 01:19:39.170
And specifically creative,

01:19:39.170 --> 01:19:41.660
not just a friend duo but a creative duo.

01:19:41.660 --> 01:19:44.480
That middle school is formative.

01:19:44.480 --> 01:19:45.610
Yeah.

01:19:45.610 --> 01:19:46.960
All right, what's your rec?

01:19:47.900 --> 01:19:51.450
My rec is a rec that you recommended to me

01:19:51.450 --> 01:19:54.390
that I finally watched over the weekend.

01:19:54.390 --> 01:19:55.687
Oh?

01:19:55.687 --> 01:19:56.854
"Chillin' Island".

01:19:58.760 --> 01:20:01.800
HBO Max is, they got some good stuff.

01:20:01.800 --> 01:20:04.160
So when I see something on HBO Max,

01:20:04.160 --> 01:20:05.180
I might give that a shot,

01:20:05.180 --> 01:20:08.770
especially if it's featuring
